# Origin:  none -- Veil-authored. T01 found no counterpart for this surface in
#          clearcotelabs/clearcote-browser (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.5 lists `972` among the
#          eight with none), and its `040-fonts` does not touch generic resolution or
#          `queryLocalFonts()` at all.
# Source:  n/a -- authored in this repository.
# Commit:  n/a -- see this repository's git log for this file.
# License: BSD-3-Clause (see CREDITS.md), same terms as the code it edits.
# Changes: n/a -- not a port.
#
# TWO holes in the font surface, both left open by `006` + `906` and both handed here with a
# measurement (6.8):
#
# 1. **The generics collapse.** Measured on the shipping engine under the `persona` case: `monospace`
#    and `serif` both report 1009x80 -- a machine whose fixed-width font is Times New Roman, which is a
#    defect on every `<pre>` a page renders as well as a tell. The cause is not a bug in `906` but its
#    scope, and it is narrower than 6.8 predicted: `--lang=en-US` moves Chrome's own font preferences to
#    the English set, so the host's families are already `Times New Roman`, `Arial` and `Consolas` --
#    and `Consolas` is a ClearType face no macOS table lists, so `906` hides it and `monospace` falls
#    through to the standard family. (The localised names 6.8 describes hide all three the same way on a
#    host whose UI locale is not English, which is the `default` case rather than a persona.) `906`'s own
#    comment names `FontSelector` as the fix, which is what this patch does: on a cross-platform claim the four
#    generics `font_persona.h` speaks for resolve to the faces the *claimed* platform's Chrome defaults
#    to. The table is Chromium's own, from `chrome/app/resources/locale_settings_mac.grd` and
#    `locale_settings_linux.grd`, with one deviation (`Courier` for `Menlo`) and three abstentions
#    (`cursive`, `fantasy`, `math`), each argued in that header.
#
#    What the page can observe is metrics, never the resolved family name, so the goal is not "the
#    exact widths of a Mac's Times" -- impossible on a host without the face, and unchanged as `906`'s
#    residual -- but that the generics *differ from each other* and that `monospace` is monospaced.
#    `Times` and `Helvetica` reach real host faces through Blink's own aliasing (`Times New Roman`,
#    `Arial`), which is why they were chosen over any other pair the claimed table lists.
#
# 2. **`queryLocalFonts()` routes around the whole block.** It reads a table the *browser* process
#    built from the real operating system, so it answers "which fonts does this machine have" with the
#    host's set while every CSS lookup answers with the claimed platform's -- and a page can ask both
#    in one function. Filtered here by the same membership rule `006`/`906` apply, from the same tables:
#    `veil_font_claim.h` declares the predicate and `font_cache.cc` defines it, because that is where
#    the three 541-name tables already are. Filtered rather than removed: the function is already
#    visible on this engine (`queryLocalFontsAvailable: true` in every capture), and deleting a
#    function a page can see is a larger claim than answering it consistently.
#
# `queryLocalFonts()` needs a user gesture, page visibility and the `local-fonts` permission, so the
# committed probe cannot call it -- it records availability only, deliberately, because a capture that
# prompts hangs. The measurement for this half is therefore taken outside the harness, with CDP
# granting the permission and evaluating under a user gesture, and it is written into 6.16.
#
# No switch is added. Both halves follow `--fingerprint` and `--fingerprint-platform`, and
# `--disable-spoofing=font` opts both out -- the same key `006` and `906` already answer to, because
# this is the same surface.

